package android.hardware.tetheroffload.control@1.1;

import @1.0::IOffloadControl;

/**
 * Interface used to control the lifecycle of tethering offload
 */
interface IOffloadControl extends @1.0::IOffloadControl {
  /**
   * Instruct hardware to send a callback after quota bytes have been transferred in either
   * direction on this upstream interface and/or stop forwarding traffic.
   *
   * The quota must be applied to all traffic on the given upstream interface that can be seen on
   * tethering offload hardware. IPv4 and IPv6 traffic both count towards the same quota. IP
   * headers are included in the byte count quota, but, link-layer headers are not.
   *
   * This API may only be called while offload is occurring on this upstream.  The hardware
   * management process is not expected to cache the value and apply the quota once offload is
   * started.  This cache is not expected, because the quota value would likely become stale over
   * time and would not reflect any new traffic that has occurred.
   *
   * The quota and {@code isLimit} must replace any previous quota and {@code isLimit} on the same
   * interface. It may be interpreted as "tell me when <quota> bytes have been transferred (in
   * either direction) on <upstream>, starting now and counting from zero."
   *
   * Once the quota is reached, the callback registered in initOffload must be called to indicate
   * this event. If {@code isLimit} is true, all offload must be stopped If offload is desired
   * again, the hardware management process must be completely reprogrammed by calling
   * setUpstreamParameters and addDownstream again. Note that it is not necessary to call
   * initOffload again to resume offload if stopOffload was not called by the client.
   *
   * @param upstream Upstream interface name that quota must apply to.
   * @param quota    The quota defined as the number of bytes, starting from zero and counting from
   *                 now.
   * @param isLimit  Indicate that if the tethering offload hardware should stop upon reaching this
   *                 event.
   *
   * @return success true if quota is applied, false otherwise
   * @return errMsg a human readable string if error has occurred.
   *
   * @todo decide which one to expose and delete the other.
   */
  setDataQuota(string upstream, uint64_t quota, bool isLimit)
          generates (bool success, string errMsg);

  /**
   * Instruct hardware to send a callback after quota bytes have been transferred in either
   * direction.
   *
   * The quota must be applied to all traffic on the tethering offload hardware.  IPv4 and IPv6
   * traffic both count towards the same quota.  IP headers are included in the byte count quota,
   * but, link-layer headers are not.
   *
   * This API may only be called while offload is occurring.  The hardware management process is not
   * expected to cache the value and apply the quota once offload is started.  This cache is not
   * expected, because the quota value would likely become stale over time and would not reflect any
   * new traffic that has occurred.
   *
   * The quota must replace any previous call with any quota specified. It may be interpreted as
   * "tell me when <quota> bytes have been transferred (in either direction), starting now and
   * counting from zero."
   *
   * Once the quota is reached, the callback registered in initOffload must be called to indicate
   * this event. Note that it is not necessary to call initOffload again to resume offload if
   * stopOffload was not called by the client.
   *
   * @param quota The quota defined as the number of bytes, starting from zero and counting from
   *              now.
   *
   * @return success true if quota is applied, false otherwise
   * @return errMsg a human readable string if error has occurred.
   *
   * @todo decide which one to expose and delete the other.
   */
  setDataAlert(uint64_t quota) generates (bool success, string errMsg);
};
